# Private Drizzle model proposal

`emailReadIntents.ts` is a concrete, unadopted schema proposal for the adjacent
`0102_mail_read_intents.sql` draft, identified in `../prepared/PREPARED-TESTS.md`
as SHA-256 `390fdee27409509e046352ab1f575a2a9a74a7a1170a197ff0beed7a8b8ad124`.
It was prepared by reading that SQL and the installed repository/Drizzle DSL.
It has not been compiled, generated, introspected or executed.

The proposal reproduces all 17 columns, nullability, the UUID and prepared-state
defaults, the actor/operation primary key, both parent foreign keys and the
thread/account composite foreign key. All three foreign keys use `ON DELETE
RESTRICT`; their update action stays the SQL default `NO ACTION`. The SQL adds
no separate mailbox-owner foreign key and no globally unique operation UUID,
so the model adds neither. Its 12 checks and three indexes match the SQL
predicates, including the unique prepared/admitted/unknown actor/thread barrier.
Explicit constraint names follow PostgreSQL's default names for this draft's
inline checks and ordered unnamed table checks. Main must reconcile those names
with the canonical snapshot at landing.

All four timestamps use `timestamp(..., { withTimezone: true, mode: "string" })`
with PostgreSQL's default precision and no application/default clock. This keeps
database microseconds in the inferred row metadata. `StoredReadIntent` already
accepts `string | Date` timestamps (nullable for admission/terminal timestamps),
so string rows fit its timestamp contract without conversion. Only the public
receipt serializer converts `admitBefore` to ISO milliseconds. Admission expiry,
marker watermarks and every authority comparison must remain in SQL. The model
imports concrete parent schema modules and no email-domain modules or schema
barrel, avoiding a new schema/domain runtime import cycle.

The mutation trigger and statement-level TRUNCATE trigger are SQL-only. The
model does not claim to enforce prepared-only insertion, immutable fingerprints
or claims, allowed transitions, terminal/unknown disposition, database-clock
admission cutoff, unresolved deletion protection, or TRUNCATE/MVCC fencing.
Those behaviors require the reviewed forward SQL and real qualification.

Main owns canonical schema/index export, migration lineage and snapshot
reconciliation, plus the unresolved privacy/erasure handling implied by RESTRICT.
This ignored proposal changes none of those and is not an installed model or
migration qualification result.
